# Dev-only, machine-specific paths (points at global pi install)
tsconfig.json

node_modules/
*.log
.DS_Store

# Host-local routing config and any credential-bearing local file. Real copies
# live in ~/.paseo-pi-team/ — these patterns only guard against accidental
# in-repo copies. `*.local.json` already covers model-routing.local.json and
# cluster-routing.local.json, so they are not listed separately.
*.local.json
auth.json

# More credential-bearing names the pack reads or writes (provider key files
# such as pi-provider.env, models.json, kubeconfig-style dotfiles) and the
# leftovers of its own writes: every config rewrite keeps a plaintext
# <name>.bak-<epoch> beside the original, and a crash can leave a *.tmp-* sibling.
# Neither is covered by the *.local.json / auth.json rules above (a backup of
# an ignored file has a different name). Same purpose as above: a guard against
# an accidental `git add .` in a directory where one was dropped, not a place
# to keep them.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.tmp-*
models.json
secrets.*
credentials.json
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
.npmrc
.netrc
# Audit trail of permit decisions (carries the OS username) and Claude session state.
permit-audit.jsonl
claude-sessions/
# Shipped examples follow <name>.example.<ext> (config/pi-models.example.json); the
# *.env and secrets.* rules above would swallow the same convention. These must
# stay AFTER them: a negation only re-includes what an earlier pattern ignored.
!*.example.env
!*.env.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template
!secrets.example.*

# Per-session scratch written by the coding agents that work in this repo:
# `.zcode/plans/` holds one plan file per session, `.claude/worktrees/` holds
# throwaway worktrees. Both are machine- and session-local, and neither is
# project content. `.claude/` is listed even though it is currently invisible
# to `git status` — git skips EMPTY directories, so it stays quiet only until
# the next session drops a file in it.
.zcode/
.claude/

# Built policy core. `extensions/paseo-team-core/*.ts` is the source; `npm run
# build:core` emits the `.js` beside it because Node refuses to strip types under
# `node_modules`, so an installed `pteam` can only load the built variant
# (ERR_UNSUPPORTED_NODE_MODULES_TYPE_STRIPPING). The `.js` IS committed: a git-tag
# install (`pteam update` is `npm i -g github:<slug>#<tag>`) has no toolchain
# and no prepare step, so the tag itself must carry the built core. CI fails if
# the committed `.js` drifts from its `.ts`. Never edit the `.js` by hand.
*.tsbuildinfo
